[ system ]
## Task
One person in Skyrim has decided to do something they cannot do openly. You
write down what they want and the steps they will take to get it.

The decision that this person is scheming has ALREADY BEEN MADE. Do not
second-guess it, do not refuse, and do not decide they are too decent or too
minor for it. Ordinary people scheme, and most schemes are small.

## Who

**{{ mastermind.name }}**{% if mastermind.standing %}, {{ mastermind.standing }}{% endif %}.
{% if mastermind.hold %}They live in {{ mastermind.hold }}.{% endif %}

{% if memories %}
### What is on their mind

These are their own recent memories, newest first. The scheme should come out
of something here — a slight they have not forgotten, a debt, a rivalry, an
opportunity they noticed. That is where a real motive comes from.

{% for m in memories %}- {{ m }}
{% endfor %}
{% else %}
This person has no recent memories on record. Build the scheme from who they
are and who is around them instead.
{% endif %}

## What you may name

You do not invent people, places or objects. Everything you can name is listed
below with a number, and you refer to it by that number. **A name that is not
on a list does not exist**, and a plan that reaches for one is thrown away.

### People
{% for a in actors %}{{ loop.index0 }}. {{ a.name }} — {{ a.relation }}{% if a.standing %}, {{ a.standing }}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}

{% if items %}
### Objects
{% for i in items %}{{ loop.index0 }}. {{ i.name }} ({{ i.category }})
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

{% if factions %}
### Organisations
{% for f in factions %}{{ loop.index0 }}. {{ f.name }}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

## The steps you may use

A plan is made of these and nothing else. Use the `id` exactly as written.

{% for s in step_types %}- `{{ s.id }}` — {{ s.description }}
{% endfor %}

## How to build the plan

The plan is **{{ min_steps }} to {{ max_steps }} steps**, in the order they
happen. The LAST step is the objective — the thing that, once done, means the
scheme succeeded. Everything before it is preparation.

A good plan reads as one thing leading to another. Find out where the ledger
is, then take it, then make sure nobody knows who did. A plan whose steps
could be shuffled without anyone noticing is a list, not a scheme.

Each step names one target by number:

- `acquire` takes its target from **Objects**.
- Every other step takes its target from **People**.

Do not use the same person for every step. A scheme with one victim and one
tool is thinner than one that touches three people who each want different
things.

## Output Format
Return a JSON object. No markdown fences. No prose before or after the object.

- `ambition` — one sentence, in the third person, saying what this person
  wants and why they will not ask for it openly. This is the only free text
  you write; it is what a reader sees first. No names of people or places that
  are not on the lists above.
- `plan` — array of {{ min_steps }} to {{ max_steps }} objects, in order. Each
  has:
  - `type` — one of the step ids above, exactly as written.
  - `target` — a number from **People**, or from **Objects** when the type is
    `acquire`.

Example shape only — do not copy its content:

```
{"ambition": "She wants the mine's ledger, because what it records would ruin her brother if anyone read it aloud.",
 "plan": [{"type": "locate", "target": 2},
          {"type": "acquire", "target": 0},
          {"type": "conceal", "target": 2}]}
```

Write plainly. No markdown, no headings, no bullet points inside `ambition`.
